Why Agents Are Soon Only Going to Have 1 Tool
November 1, 20269 minute read
For the past two years, building capable Agents meant designing tools, figuring out which actions help the model complete a task, deciding how granular each tool schema needs to be, or iterating on system instruction templates. This is changing. Agents are moving in the opposite direction by collapsing their entire tool catalog into one tool: exec.
This is called Code Mode (or Programmatic Tool Calling). Instead of forcing the model to trigger tools one at a time across turns, the harness gives the model a single exec(code) tool. The model writes a short TypeScript or Python script that chains, combines, and awaits actions inside an isolated sandbox.
From single actions to chaining workflows in code
To understand why agents are converging on a single exec tool, we should look at how tool calling evolved.
Initially, we only gave agents a handful of tools because they couldn't reliably choose or filter across a large catalog. As models improved, we connected dozens of tools or MCP servers, but ran into context window limits. We solved that with tool search and progressive disclosure, letting agents look up tools on demand.
Even with dynamic tool loading, we still had dozens of individual tools, and were still stuck with the limitation of standard tool calling: one action per turn.
In standard tool calling, the model generates a structured object (typically JSON) that defines a single action (or at best a flat batch of independent calls, like reading three files), waits for the raw output to come back, adds it into the prompt history, reasons again, and decides what to do next.
From my own experience, we made a big jump recently in how reliably models can combine and chain dependent actions in a single pass:
Research and filtering: Instead of fetching a list of sources in Turn 1, reading them in Turn 2, and cross-referencing them in Turn 3, the model can write a script that queries the sources, filters out irrelevant hits, and extracts the exact answer in one step.
Multi-file edits and inline verification: Standard edit tools typically operate on one file per tool call. With Code Mode, the model can apply coordinated changes across multiple files, run a verification check, inspect the output, or branch on pass or fail inside the same script.
Browser and computer use: In UI automation, the chain of actions is often obvious: click a button, fill a form, wait for a selector, or paginate through a table in a loop. Instead of paying a full model round-trip for every click or keystroke, the model can script the entire sequence or loop in one turn and only return when the page state changes.
Parallel fan-out and sub-agents: When a task requires running 20 lookups or calling specialist sub-models in parallel and feeding those outputs into a second API, the model can await Promise.all(...), transform the array, and call the next function without round-tripping through the LLM context window between steps.
Several shifts came together to enable this. Frontier models are trained heavily on code, and providers have started post-training models for Code Mode. At the same time, MCP servers now increasingly ship outputSchema alongside inputSchema, giving the model upfront knowledge of return types so it can chain Tool A into Tool B inside the same script before seeing runtime output.
How Code Mode works
Code Mode collapses the tool catalog into a single tool on the model surface: exec(code).
Instead of passing 80 separate JSON Schema tool objects to the model API, the agent compiles those schemas into compact type declarations (declare namespace tools { ... }). When the model needs to combine actions across external systems, it calls exec and writes a script that invokes await tools.<server>.<tool>(args).
TypeScript// Pseudo-code: TypeScript declarations exposed to the model
declare namespace tools {
namespace linear {
function listProjects(): Promise<Array<{ id: string; name: string }>>;
function listIssues(args: { projectId: string }): Promise<Array<{ id: string; title: string; assignee?: string }>>;
function archiveIssue(args: { id: string }): Promise<{ ok: boolean }>;
}
}
// Optional: when wrapping hundreds of tools, omit full namespaces upfront and expose
// a global map in the sandbox that the model can iterate and filter over in code
declare const ALL_TOOLS: Record<string, { description: string; signature: string }>;
// Multimodal output helpers: only what is passed here enters the model context window
declare function text(output: string): void;
declare function image(data: string | Uint8Array, mimeType?: string): void;
declare function audio(data: string | Uint8Array, mimeType?: string): void;
// Cross-turn state helpers: persist data in host memory without paying token costs
declare function store(key: string, value: unknown): void;
declare function load<T = unknown>(key: string): T;
To save context across a large catalog, we don't have to expose every tool directly. We can leverage principles we already built, like tool search, or expose a global ALL_TOOLS map inside the sandbox. Similarly, multimodal output helpers (text(), image(), audio()) let the script pass text, images, or audio directly back into the model's context window.
TypeScript// Pseudo-code: What the model emits inside a single exec() call
const projects = await tools.linear.listProjects();
const issueBatches = await Promise.all(
projects.map((p) => tools.linear.listIssues({ projectId: p.id }))
);
const unassigned = issueBatches.flat().filter((issue) => !issue.assignee);
await Promise.all(unassigned.map((issue) => tools.linear.archiveIssue({ id: issue.id })));
text(`Archived ${unassigned.length} unassigned issues across ${projects.length} projects.`);
The sandbox executes all 55 underlying API calls, filters the payloads in memory, and returns only the one-line text() string ("Archived 42 unassigned issues across 12 projects.") to the model's context window.
Why a JS/Python sandbox instead of just bash?
You can build a tool proxy in bash too, but orchestrating tools inside a language runtime like JavaScript or Python can bring benefits that are harder to achieve in bash environments.
Typed functions: MCP servers and internal APIs map directly to typed await tools.<service>.<method>(args) calls with native objects, Promise.all, and try/catch error handling instead of fragile bash string escaping.
No CLI exploration tax: When given custom CLIs or scripts in bash, models tend to over-explore before acting, burning turns and tokens running --help across subcommands or reading script source code just to figure out flags and output formats. Typed interface definitions give the agent a contract upfront so it can act immediately.
Multimodal context: A shell only returns stdout text. If a multimodal model wants to inspect an image, it cannot run cat screenshot.png, which is why bash harnesses still had to bolt on separate read or view_media tools. In an exec sandbox, calling image(screenshot) or audio(clip) tells the host runtime to attach the actual media part directly to the model's next turn.
Credential and policy proxy: Every await tools.* call crosses a structured bridge to the host runtime, which can inject OAuth tokens outside the sandbox, validate schemas, or pause for user confirmation on destructive calls.
State management: In standard tool calling, the prompt history is the only state store, so reusing a large query result in Turn 2 means keeping the raw JSON in the context window on every turn. Exposing a KV interface lets the agent persist state outside of the context window across turns.
Building a 1-tool agent with the Gemini Interactions API
Here is a minimal, runnable pseudo example that provides gemini-4-argon with a single execution tool.
TypeScriptimport vm from "node:vm";
import { GoogleGenAI } from "@google/genai";
const client = new GoogleGenAI({});
// Underlying host functions (e.g., MCP clients or internal APIs)
const tools = {
listPRFiles: async ({ pr }: { pr: number }) => [
{ path: "src/auth.ts", additions: 120 },
{ path: "src/payments.ts", additions: 85 },
{ path: "src/utils.ts", additions: 12 },
{ path: "README.md", additions: 60 },
],
runSecurityScan: async ({ path }: { path: string }) => ({
findings: path.includes("auth") ? ["Missing token expiry check"] : [],
}),
postPRComment: async ({ pr, body }: { pr: number; body: string }) => ({ posted: true }),
};
// 1. Expose a single `exec` tool with TypeScript .d.ts signatures
const execTool = {
type: "function" as const,
name: "exec",
description: `Run an async JavaScript body in an isolated sandbox (no imports; call text() to output).
Available globals:
declare namespace tools {
function listPRFiles(args: { pr: number }): Promise<Array<{ path: string; additions: number }>>;
function runSecurityScan(args: { path: string }): Promise<{ findings: string[] }>;
function postPRComment(args: { pr: number; body: string }): Promise<{ posted: boolean }>;
}
declare function text(summary: string): void;`,
parameters: {
type: "object",
properties: { code: { type: "string" } },
required: ["code"],
},
};
// 2. Turn 1: Gemini 4 Argon emits a single script to fetch, filter, scan, and comment
const turn1 = await client.interactions.create({
model: "gemini-4-argon",
input: "Scan all modified .ts files with >50 additions in PR #42, post a PR comment with any findings, and output a 1-line summary.",
tools: [execTool],
});
// 3. Run the script in a separate context (use QuickJS or isolated-vm for untrusted code)
const fcStep = turn1.steps?.find((s: any) => s.type === "function_call") as any;
const output: string[] = [];
await vm.runInNewContext(`(async () => {\n${fcStep.arguments.code}\n})()`, {
tools,
text: (msg: string) => output.push(msg),
});
// 4. Turn 2: Send only the 1-line summary back via previous_interaction_id
const turn2 = await client.interactions.create({
model: "gemini-4-argon",
previous_interaction_id: turn1.id,
tools: [execTool],
input: [
{
type: "function_result",
name: fcStep.name,
call_id: fcStep.id,
result: [{ type: "text", text: output.join("\n") }],
},
],
});
console.log(turn2.output_text);
When vm.runInNewContext executes the script generated by gemini-4-argon:
The script calls await tools.listPRFiles({ pr: 42 }) and filters the list in memory down to the .ts files with more than 50 additions (files.filter(f => f.path.endsWith(".ts") && f.additions > 50)).
It runs tools.runSecurityScan on just those matching files, checks whether any findings exist, posts the PR comment via tools.postPRComment, and emits a single line via text().
None of the intermediate file objects or raw scanner payloads ever enter the gemini-4-argon context window; only the one-line output summary is sent back in Turn 2.
Agent harnesses are already defaulting to Code Mode
Across the ecosystem, coding agents and developer frameworks are converging on Code Mode as their default way to orchestrate tools.
Harnesses like Pi, Factory Droid, and OpenAI Codex now ship with or default to Code Mode. Factory switched Droid 1.0 to Code Mode, improving their Terminal-Bench 4 accuracy by +3.5 points and cutting model spend by 15%.
Agent frameworks like Pydantic AI, LangChain, or Cloudflare's Agents SDK support similar Code Mode tools already.
What comes next
Does this mean every agent will soon only have a single exec tool? Not necessarily. This is as much a research and product question as an agent harness engineering one.
When you start experimenting with Code Mode, you will also quickly notice that many of the tools, CLIs, and Agent Skills we built might not integrate cleanly out of the box because they were optimized for bash and human-readable text rather than typed code execution.
This might force you to take a step back and audit what your agent actually needs. Happy building!

AI agents that interact with enterprise APIs on behalf of users need to maintain user identity throughout the request chain. Without identity propagation, API calls arrive at downstream services as the application itself rather than the user who initiated the action. This breaks audit trails, bypasses user-level access controls, and violates the principle of least privilege.
Amazon Bedrock AgentCore is a platform to build, connect, and optimize agents at scale, with any framework or model. This post shows how to configure AgentCore Gateway, a capability of Amazon Bedrock AgentCore, for an on-behalf-of (OBO) token exchange with Microsoft Entra ID. An AI agent can then call the ServiceNow REST API while preserving the calling user’s identity. You configure the token exchange on the gateway target, and AgentCore Gateway performs the exchange on each request. The pattern can be applied to other REST APIs protected by OAuth.
In production, you can additionally pair identity propagation with Amazon Bedrock Guardrails to filter harmful content and validate the agent’s inputs and outputs.
Solution overview
The architecture involves three key components connected through AgentCore Gateway:
An AI agent application, protected by Microsoft Entra ID through an OAuth application registration.
A Model Context Protocol (MCP) server, implemented using AgentCore Gateway, sitting between the agent and ServiceNow.
The ServiceNow REST API, configured with an inbound OAuth integration tied to Microsoft Entra ID.
The following figure shows the end-to-end solution architecture, including components within AWS, Microsoft Entra ID, and ServiceNow.
Figure 1: Solution architecture across AWS, Microsoft Entra ID, and ServiceNow
As shown in Figure 1, the user acquires a JSON Web Token (JWT) to invoke the agent 1. The agent authenticates to the gateway with a JWT token scoped to the agent’s own application registration 2. The gateway then exchanges that token, using the OBO flow, for a new token scoped to the ServiceNow API connector, with the original user’s identity preserved 3. ServiceNow receives a valid token whose preferred_username claim maps to an existing ServiceNow user and applies that user’s permissions to the API call 4.
How OBO token exchange works in this context
When the agent calls the gateway, it presents a JWT with an audience claim matching the agent’s client ID (for example, 3ff88e17-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXXXXXXXXXX). ServiceNow, however, expects a token with an audience claim matching its own API connector client ID (5d4a7a11-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXXXXXXXXXX).
The OBO flow bridges this gap. AgentCore Gateway sends the inbound token to the Entra token endpoint along with the agent application’s client credentials, the target scope, and a parameter requested_token_use: on_behalf_of. Entra validates that the calling user consented to the delegation, then issues a new token for the ServiceNow API with the original user’s claims intact.
Prerequisites
Before you begin, you need the following:
An AWS account with access to AgentCore Gateway.
A Microsoft Entra ID tenant with permissions to create application registrations. Your account needs at least the Application Developer role to register applications.
A ServiceNow instance with administrative access to configure inbound OAuth integrations. You can create a ServiceNow personal developer instance (PDI) at the ServiceNow personal developer instance signup page https://developer.servicenow.com/.
Two Entra application registrations: one representing the AI agent, and one representing the ServiceNow API connector.
Configuring Microsoft Entra ID
Start by creating an application registration to represent the ServiceNow API connector. The following screenshot shows the application registration page for the ServiceNow API Connector in Microsoft Entra ID.
Figure 2: Application registration for the ServiceNow API connector in Microsoft Entra ID
After creating, in the Overview page note the Application (client) ID.
In the navigation menu expand Manage and select Expose an API and add a scope named user_impersonation using the default Application ID URI. This scope is what the OBO flow requests when exchanging tokens. The following screenshot shows how to expose an API in Microsoft Entra ID.
Figure 3: Exposing the user_impersonation scope in Microsoft Entra ID
Next, create an application registration for the AI agent. The following screenshot shows the application registration page in Microsoft Entra ID for the ServiceNow Agent application.
Figure 4: Application registration for the ServiceNow Agent in Microsoft Entra ID
Note the Application (client) ID for the ServiceNowAgent.
Create a client secret on this registration. The gateway needs these credentials to perform the OBO exchange. The following screenshot shows how you can create a client secret on the ServiceNow Agent application registration in Microsoft Entra ID.
Figure 5: Creating a client secret on the ServiceNow Agent application registration
Then configure the agent application to have API permissions to call the ServiceNow API connector application. The following screenshot shows the API permissions that you need to configure on the ServiceNow Agent application registration in Microsoft Entra ID.
Figure 6: API permission configuration in Microsoft Entra ID
No changes to the Entra configuration are needed beyond what you would set up for any standard OBO integration between two applications.
Configuring ServiceNow
In the ServiceNow administrative console, navigate to System OAuth > Inbound Integrations and create a new inbound integration (Third party ID token issued by OIDC supporting identity provider). Specify the client ID from the ServiceNow API connector application registration you created in Entra. The following screenshot shows the configuration of the inbound integration in ServiceNow.
Figure 7: Configuring the inbound integration in ServiceNow
In the same screen, create a new OpenID Connect (OIDC) provider configuration and enter the OIDC metadata URL for your Entra tenant. Map the user claim to the appropriate user field. For example, if your Entra tenant places the user’s email in the preferred_username claim, map that to the Email field in ServiceNow. Clear JTI verification if your Entra tenant doesn’t include it in tokens. Entra uses an internal claim named uti on tokens it issues, which is equivalent to JTI. Consider mapping this uti claim to a jti claim to provide additional replay protection and denylisting. The following screenshot shows how to configure the OIDC provider in ServiceNow.
Figure 8: Configuring the OAuth OIDC provider in ServiceNow
Finally, in the same screen, create an auth scope for the integration (for example, APIConnector). The following screenshot shows how to create a new auth scope in ServiceNow.
Figure 9: Creating a new auth scope in ServiceNow
Limit the auth scope to the Table API, or whichever ServiceNow APIs your agent needs to access, as shown in the following screenshot.
Figure 10: Limiting the auth scope to the Table API in ServiceNow
After this configuration is complete, ServiceNow accepts authorization tokens with an audience of your API connector’s client ID and resolves the calling user from the preferred_username claim.
Configuring AgentCore Gateway
With Entra and ServiceNow configured, you can now set up AgentCore Gateway to handle the OBO exchange natively.
Step 1: Create an outbound auth configuration
Create a new outbound auth configuration (choose Build > Identity > Add Outbound Auth > Add OAuth client) in AgentCore and select Customized provider. Enter the client ID and client secret from your agent application registration, along with the Entra discovery URL as shown in the following screenshot.
Figure 11: Adding an OAuth client in AgentCore Gateway
Expand Additional configurations and select JWT authorization grant as shown in the following screenshot. This tells the gateway to use a JWT-based grant type for token exchange.
Figure 12: Configuring the JWT authorization grant
Step 2: Create the gateway with MCP version 2025-11-25
Create a new AgentCore Gateway (Build > Gateways > Create gateway). When defining the gateway details, expand Additional configurations — optional and select MCP version 2025-11-25 as shown in the following screenshot. This version is required for native OBO support. You can’t turn on native OBO for gateways created with earlier MCP versions.
Figure 13: Selecting MCP version 2025-11-25
Configure the inbound identity using your agent’s application registration (select Use existing identity provider configurations), as you would for any gateway that accepts Entra-authenticated requests.
Under JWT Authorization Configuration add the Application ID we documented earlier for the ServiceNowAgent as shown in the following screenshot.
Figure 14: Configuring inbound authorization in AgentCore Gateway
Step 3: Add a target with token exchange
Add a target to the gateway pointing to your ServiceNow REST endpoint. Define the schema inline. For example, to expose a tool called ListIncidents that calls the ServiceNow Table API, you can use the following OpenAPI specification (replace the server URL with that for your PDI).
